lp, but W9 actively reduces suits.\n\nHence, the best discard is W9.", "output": "Evaluation: Your hand contains a nearly complete T7-8-9 chow and a B3-4-5 chow with a B5 pair, providing strong foundations for a standard hand. Key opportunities include pursuing Pure Straight (requires T1-2-3/4-5-6/7-8-9) or accumulating smaller patterns like All Chows (2 points) and One Voided Suit (1 point). However, W9 is a terminal tile in a third suit (Characters), disrupting potential One Voided Suit (needing exactly two suits) and offering minimal synergy with your T/B-focused structure. Discarding W9 streamlines your hand toward two suits (Bamboo/Dots), enabling One Voided Suit (1 point) and improving flexibility for chow-based patterns. Alternatives like discarding B8 or honors (F1/F2/J2) retain less strategic value\u2014B8 is isolated in Dots, while single honors rarely yield meaningful points without pungs. Prioritizing suit consolidation maximizes scoring potential while minimizing deadweight tiles.\n\nReason: W9 is the weakest tile due to its role in a third suit with no supporting tiles, whereas keeping T/B tiles preserves pathways to Pure Straight components (e.g., T4-5-6) and multi-chow patterns. Eliminating W9 secures One Voided Suit (1 point) immediately upon discard and aligns with Chicken Hand fallback requirements (8 points minimum). Other discards either fragment potential melds (e.g., B8) or sacrifice negligible honor-based opportunities (F1/F2/J2).\n\nAnswer: Play W9"}
